/// Chapter 1 of the guide, written from md-docs/01-introduction.md by
/// scripts/site-from-guide.py; edit the guide, not this file.
page IntroductionGuide(path: "/docs/guide/introduction", title: "Introduction", description: "What WebFluent is, the five ideas behind it, when to use it and when not to, and how the guide is organised by what you need.", layout: DocsShell("Introduction", chapter: t("guide.chapter", { n: "1" }), blurb: "What WebFluent is, what it does for you, where it fits and where it does not, and how to find your way around this guide.", source: "md-docs/01-introduction.md", sections: [Anchor(id: "a-first-page", label: "A first page"), Anchor(id: "what-you-get", label: "What you get"), Anchor(id: "the-five-ideas", label: "The five ideas"), Anchor(id: "when-to-use-webfluent", label: "When to use WebFluent"), Anchor(id: "how-this-guide-is-organised", label: "How this guide is organised"), Anchor(id: "where-to-start", label: "Where to start"), Anchor(id: "versions", label: "Versions")], nextTo: "/docs/guide/getting-started", nextLabel: "2. {t("ch.02")}")) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Markdown("WebFluent is a programming language for websites, PDFs and slide decks. You write `.wf` files that say what each page shows, the state behind it, and what happens when the reader acts. The compiler, `wf`, turns those files into a finished site — HTML, CSS and a small JavaScript runtime — or into a PDF or a slide deck from the same source.\n\nThe one thing to remember: the compiler knows every element, prop and type in your program, so most mistakes stop the build with a line number instead of failing quietly in the browser.\n\nIn this chapter:\n\n- [A first page](#a-first-page), and what each line of it does\n- [What you get](#what-you-get) from the compiler\n- [The five ideas](#the-five-ideas) the language is built on\n- [When to use WebFluent](#when-to-use-webfluent), and when to use something else\n- [How this guide is organised](#how-this-guide-is-organised), and where to start")

        Heading("A first page", id: "a-first-page").h2

        Markdown("This file is a complete page with a counter:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Home(path: "/", title: "Hello", description: "A first WebFluent page.") {
    state count = 0
    Container {
        Heading("Hello, world").h1
        Text("You have clicked {count} times.")
        Button("Click me").primary { on click { count = count + 1 } }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("- `page Home(path: \"/\", …)` declares a page and the address it answers. `title` and `description` become the browser tab's title, the search snippet and the link-preview cards.\n- `state count = 0` declares a value that can change. Everything that reads it updates when it does.\n- `Container`, `Heading`, `Text` and `Button` are built-in elements. `.h1` and `.primary` are *flags*: short switches that pick a heading level or a button's colour.\n- `on click \{ … \}` is what happens when the reader clicks the button.\n\nAfter `wf build`, the page is ready to deploy. Routing, reactivity, a styled button, accessible markup, the `<title>`, the meta description and the Open Graph tags are all in the output; you wrote none of them by hand.")

        Heading("What you get", id: "what-you-get").h2

        Markdown("- **One binary, no toolchain.** `wf` is the compiler, the dev server, the formatter, the test runner and the language server. There is no `node_modules`, no bundler and no configuration to write before the first page.\n- **A compiler that checks your work.** Every built-in element and every component you declare has a known set of props, flags, events and slots. A misspelled prop, a flag the element does not take, a field a record does not have, or a value that may be `null` read as if it were not — each is an error with a line number and a hint, never a silent no-op in the browser.\n- **Fine-grained reactivity.** A `state` is a *signal*: a value that tells its readers when it changes. Only the text node or attribute that reads it updates; nothing else re-renders.\n- **More than fifty designed components.** Layout, navigation, tables, forms, overlays and media, styled by *design tokens* (named values for colours, spacing and fonts) that a theme changes in a few lines.\n- **The web done properly by default.** Each page is pre-rendered to HTML. You get one stylesheet pruned to what you used, a runtime cut down to what the program reaches, an image pipeline, a sitemap, canonical links, Open Graph and JSON-LD, a Content-Security-Policy that the build holds its own output to, and accessibility checks on every build.\n- **Everything a site needs, built in.** Routing, stores, forms and validation, typed HTTP services, WebSockets and WebRTC, offline support with a service worker, translation with automatic right-to-left layout, animation and dark mode.\n- **More than websites.** The same language compiles to PDF documents and slide decks, renders templates with JSON data on a server, and publishes components as custom elements that any framework can use.")

        Heading("The five ideas", id: "the-five-ideas").h2

        Markdown("1. **A project is a set of declarations.** Every `.wf` file holds declarations of thirteen kinds: `page`, `component`, `store`, `theme`, `app`, `type`, `enum`, `api`, `const`, `data`, `image`, `animation` and `test`. This guide covers each one. A `.js` or `.css` file under `src/` is part of the project too. Each page names its own route, so there is no separate route table.\n2. **An element is a typed call.** In `Button(\"Save\", tone: .primary).lg \{ on click \{ save() \} \}` you see the parts in order: one positional argument, named props, `.flag`s, then a block. The compiler knows the props and cases of every built-in and of every component you declare.\n3. **State is a signal.** After `state count = 0`, anything that reads `count` follows it: `derived` values recompute, `effect`s run again, and the page updates.\n4. **Styles are CSS, written on the element.** `style \{ padding: $md; &:hover \{ background: $surface \} \}` holds raw CSS values, design tokens written with `$`, and nested rules in CSS nesting syntax.\n5. **The build is the whole site.** `wf build` writes everything a host needs, and `wf serve` rebuilds it every time you save.")

        Heading("When to use WebFluent", id: "when-to-use-webfluent").h2

        Markdown("WebFluent fits well when you want:\n\n- a marketing site, a documentation site, a blog or a portfolio that must be fast, accessible and well indexed, with some interactivity;\n- a dashboard, an admin panel or an internal tool that talks to an API;\n- invoices, reports or slide decks generated from the same data as a site;\n- to work in a small team that would rather not maintain a JavaScript build chain.\n\nUse something else when:\n\n- you depend on a large ecosystem of framework-specific UI libraries. You can still embed any library through [JavaScript interop](/docs/guide/interop), but you write the binding yourself.\n- you need server-side rendering on every request with per-user data. A WebFluent site is static files plus a browser runtime, and your server stays your server. [Server rendering](/docs/guide/server-rendering) of templates does cover emails, invoices and HTML fragments.\n- you need a native mobile app.")

        Heading("How this guide is organised", id: "how-this-guide-is-organised").h2

        Markdown("Each page of the guide is one of four kinds, and each kind answers a different need:")

        Table(caption: "How this guide is organised") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Kind").header
                    Table.Cell("Read it when you…").header
                    Table.Cell("It gives you").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("**Tutorial**") }
                    Table.Cell("are learning, and want to build something")
                    Table.Cell("a lesson with a guaranteed working result")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("**How-to guide**") }
                    Table.Cell("have a goal, such as validating a form")
                    Table.Cell("the steps to that one result")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("**Reference**") }
                    Table.Cell("need a fact, such as a prop's default")
                    Table.Cell("a complete, exact description")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("**Explanation**") }
                    Table.Cell("want to understand how something works and why")
                    Table.Cell("the ideas, the reasons and the trade-offs")
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("The guide has six parts. Each part is mostly one kind of page:")

        Table(caption: "How this guide is organised") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Part").header
                    Table.Cell("Chapters").header
                    Table.Cell("Kind").header
                    Table.Cell("What it covers").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("**Start**") }
                    Table.Cell("1–4")
                    Table.Cell("explanation (1, 4), quick start (2), tutorial (3)")
                    Table.Cell("What WebFluent is, installing it, a first site built step by step, and a map from React, Vue and Svelte")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("**The basics**") }
                    Table.Cell("5–14")
                    Table.Cell("explanation, each ending in a short reference table")
                    Table.Cell("The language itself, in the order the ideas build on each other")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("**Building**") }
                    Table.Cell("15–24")
                    Table.Cell("how-to")
                    Table.Cell("Styling, forms, data, real-time, offline, motion, translation, accessibility, security and testing")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("**Shipping**") }
                    Table.Cell("25–36")
                    Table.Cell("how-to, with explanation first in 25 and 26; chapter 36 is tutorials")
                    Table.Cell("Project structure, static or single-page, content and search, media, deploying, environments, performance, JavaScript interop, desktop apps, PDFs and slides, server rendering, and three whole-project tutorials")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("**Reference**") }
                    Table.Cell("37–44")
                    Table.Cell("reference")
                    Table.Cell("Every component, command, configuration key, diagnostic, built-in, design token, runtime function and grammar rule")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("**Help**") }
                    Table.Cell("45–49")
                    Table.Cell("troubleshooting, upgrade notes, glossary")
                    Table.Cell("Troubleshooting, upgrading, browser support, a glossary and contributing")
                }
            }
        }

        Heading("Where to start", id: "where-to-start").h2

        Markdown("- **To learn WebFluent:** read [Getting started](/docs/guide/getting-started), work through the [tutorial](/docs/guide/tutorial), then read chapters 5–12 in order. Every example in them builds. For more practice, try the [three project tutorials](/docs/tutorials).\n- **To understand it coming from a framework:** read [Coming from React, Vue or Svelte](/docs/guide/coming-from) first. It maps what you know to what is here.\n- **To build a feature:** go to the chapter in *Building* or *Shipping* that names the task.\n- **To look something up:** use the reference chapters, starting with the [components reference](/docs/reference). They are generated from the compiler, or checked against it.\n- **To fix a problem:** start at [Troubleshooting](/docs/guide/troubleshooting). Every diagnostic code has an entry in [Diagnostics](/docs/guide/diagnostics).\n\nThe test suite parses, type-checks and compiles every `wf` code block in this guide, so what you read is what the compiler accepts. A block that leaves something out says so with a `…`.")

        Heading("Versions", id: "versions").h2

        Markdown("This guide describes WebFluent 5, the stable line. Run `wf --version` to see which release you have. [Upgrading](/docs/guide/upgrading) lists what changed in each release.")
    }
}
